; ACCEPTANCE: a Win32 parameter written `TYPE *name` is a POINTER.
;
; C binds `*` to the DECLARATOR, so `_In_opt_ VOID *lp` is a pointer whose name
; is `lp` — and the survey took the last field of the parameter as the name and
; threw the star away, making the parameter a `VOID` BY VALUE. In the other
; direction it made `BOOL *pfOn` a WORD, so `GetDevicePowerState` was declared
; `(int int)`: a program could pass the literal 0 where an address belongs, the
; type checker would accept it, and the host would write a BOOL to address 0.
; 585 of the 9,427 declarations the generator wrote had a parameter of that
; shape (structval-2026-09-12).
;
; `IsBadReadPtr(_In_opt_ VOID *lp, _In_ UINT_PTR ucb)` is the witness because it
; is CALLABLE: the annotation says the pointer may be NULL, so `(x64.null)` is a
; legal argument and the program runs rather than being refused.
;
; TWO CALLS, because one could not fail. The host answers differently for the
; same pointer at two lengths, so the program cannot be right by accident:
;
;   null, 1 byte  ->  1   TRUE: address 0 is not readable
;   null, 0 bytes ->  0   FALSE: there is nothing to check
;
; Verified against C on this host before it was written here.
;
; And it does not compile against the old declaration AT ALL: with `lp` typed
; `int`, `(x.null)` is a `ptr` where an `int` is required, and with the literal
; 0 in its place the FIXED declaration refuses it. The two spellings and the two
; declarations are each other's negative.

(use win/WinBase as w)
(use x64 as x)
(use win/fmt as fmt)

(export main)

(def main (fn ()
  (seq (fmt.print-int (w.IsBadReadPtr (x.null) 1))
       (fmt.print-int (w.IsBadReadPtr (x.null) 0)))))
